  <aside class="footnote-card" id="footnote-mapped-traffic" aria-labelledby="footnote-mapped-traffic-heading">
    <div class="footnote-header">
      <h2 id="footnote-mapped-traffic-heading" class="footnote-title">
        <span class="footnote-symbol" aria-hidden="true">*</span> Why some traffic is not mapped to Baseline targets
      </h2>
      <a href="#footnote-ref-mapped-traffic" class="footnote-backlink" aria-label="Back to Mapped traffic summary">
        <span aria-hidden="true">&uarr;</span> Back to top
      </a>
    </div>
    <p class="footnote-intro">
      Roughly 9&ndash;11% of global RUM Archive beacons cannot be deterministically mapped to a specific browser release in <a href="https://github.com/web-platform-dx/baseline-browser-mapping" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><code>baseline-browser-mapping</code><span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a> because the underlying telemetry lacks a usable browser or operating system version. Rather than guessing feature support from incomplete metadata, unmapped beacons are excluded from the availability calculations:
    </p>
    <ul class="footnote-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Unidentified user agents (~4.5% of global traffic):</strong> Beacons where the browser family, rendering engine, and operating system fields are all empty in RUM Archive (commonly caused by privacy-stripped User-Agent strings, custom native app requests, or unrecognized clients).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>iOS and WebKit traffic without an OS version (~4.5% of global traffic):</strong> On iOS, web platform feature support is tied to the underlying iOS release rather than the host app version. However, several high-volume iOS User-Agent strings&mdash;including the Google Search App (<code>GSA</code>), in-app <code>WKWebView</code> instances (<code>Mobile Safari Webview</code>, Facebook, etc.), and generic WebKit requests&mdash;omit the iOS version in the aggregated dataset and only expose an app build number or frozen legacy WebKit tokens (<code>AppleWebKit/605.1.15</code>, frozen since 2018, or <code>AppleWebKit/537.36</code>, frozen since 2013). Because those tokens span many years of releases, their Baseline support cannot be inferred without an OS version.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Other unmapped clients (~0.1% of global traffic):</strong> Legacy browsers outside the Baseline core browser set (such as Internet Explorer) and beacons that report an operating system without a browser or rendering engine version.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </aside>
